WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120
Thanks for tuning in to First Principles Daily Episode 9 for June 14th, 2026.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.900
Let's break something down to its raw materials and build the reasoning back up.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:15.360
A blood test whose reagents cost pennies can still bill for hundreds because the price

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.060
lives almost entirely outside the materials.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.560
When you look at what actually happens inside the reaction, the chemicals themselves are

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.580
the smallest slice of the total expense.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:31.720
Everything else that surrounds the reaction, the machines, the people, the rules, the paperwork,

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:36.680
has grown into the main driver of what the patient or insurer ultimately pays.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:42.840
A standard panel of blood tests today can be ordered for a few hundred dollars at a commercial lab.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:49.320
That figure covers a basic metabolic panel or a comprehensive metabolic panel that checks glucose,

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:53.440
electrolytes, kidney markers, and liver enzymes all at once.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:58.480
Yet the chemical reagents that actually perform the measurements are measured in fractions

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:04.660
of a dollar per sample. Those reagents sit in small bottles or cartridges and are dispensed in

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:10.560
microleaders drops. So even when you add up every chemical that changes color or produces an electrical

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:16.820
signal, the amount used per patient remains tiny. The difference is not hidden in exotic inputs.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:21.500
No rare earth elements or custom synthesized molecules drive the cost.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:27.780
It sits in the chain of instruments, labor, regulatory steps, building systems, and liability

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:34.640
buffers that have grown around the test. First, principles reasoning asks what would remain if those

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.900
layers were stripped back to the physical minimum required to move a drop of blood across a reaction

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:45.720
and read the result. In other words, what stays when you keep only the chemistry and the simplest

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:51.700
way to read it? Diagnostic labs inherited their workflow from centralized hospital pathology

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:58.020
departments. They were built to handle low volumes of complex cases under strict regulatory oversight.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:03.940
Each sample therefore travels through a sessioning where it is logged and labeled then

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:07.880
allocating, where it is split into smaller portions for different machines.

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:14.340
After that comes placement on high throughput analyzers, quality control runs that check whether

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:20.480
the machines are still accurate that day, pathologists review in ambiguous cases, and finally an

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:26.140
electronic report routed through multiple intermediators before it reaches the ordering physician.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:32.580
Every handoff adds documentation requirements whose purpose is defensible records rather than

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:39.260
lower cost. Reembursement rules further entrench the structure. Payers reimburse line items that match

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:45.720
establish CPT codes so labs price to the code rather than to the marginal cost of the next test.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:52.380
Capital equipment is amortized over years. The service contracts that keep analyzers calibrated

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:55.640
add steady overhead that does not shrink with volume.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:02.000
Fragmented ordering practices mean most labs still receive specimens in batches that arrive at

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:09.040
irregular times. This forces them to maintain excess analyzer capacity and train staff on standby.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:14.600
The result is a delivered price that feels normal inside the industry even though the variable

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:21.300
material cost per test has fallen for decades. Safety and traceability rules are non-negotiable,

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:27.400
yet they are implemented through layers of process that were designed when sample volumes were smaller

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:33.620
and data systems were less capable. The same regulatory framework that prevents errors also

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:39.160
prevents rapid substitution of lower cost equipment or simplified workflows. In short,

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:44.880
the price reflects an accumulated set of choices about risk allocation, reimbursement mechanics,

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:51.940
and capital utilization rather than the chemistry itself. A rough magic wand estimate begins with the

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:58.240
reagents. A typical color metric or enzymatic assay for glucose, electrolytes, or liver enzymes

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:04.660
uses microleaders scale volumes of commodity chemicals whose bulk prices are measured in dollars

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:12.100
per liter, even allowing for stabilizers, calibrators, and controls. The raw material cost per test

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:18.220
lands on the order of a few cents to perhaps 20 cents when purchased at scale. Adding the plastic

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:24.380
consumable, a single-use crevette or microfluidic card might bring the total physical inputs to roughly

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:30.580
50 cents on an optimistic estimate. A more conservative accounting that includes the cost of the

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:36.380
blood collection tube and the small amount of deionized water or buffer still keeps the floor well

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:42.720
under two dollars. Against a commonly observed build price of 100 to several hundred dollars for

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:49.140
a basic metabolic or comprehensive metabolic panel, the implied idiot index therefore sits

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:55.620
somewhere between 50 and several hundred. That ratio is not a measured fact, it is an order of

00:04:55.620 --> 00:05:02.420
magnitude signal that the dominant costs lie elsewhere. The gap opens in several distinct stages.

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:09.240
First, the analyzer itself must be purchased, validated, and maintained under CLIA or equivalent

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:15.740
rules. Those fixed costs are spread across thousands of tests but still appear in the per test price.

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:22.600
Second, each sample requires trained personnel to verify identity, handle exceptions, and document

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:28.140
chain of custody, and activity whose labor minutes do not shrink linearly with automation.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:35.060
Third, billing and collections consume additional administrative effort because reimbursement pathways

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:42.560
differ by payer and often require prior authorization or appeals. Fourth, the regulatory and liability

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:48.840
buffer adds another increment. Every test is run with extra controls and retained records whose

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:54.780
marginal cost is small but whose cumulative effect on staffing and legal overhead is material.

00:05:55.420 --> 00:06:00.760
These layers compound because each one is sized to the risk tolerance of the most conservative

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:07.040
participant in the chain rather than to the physics of the reaction. A redesign that started from

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:12.850
reagent floor would first attack the instrument and workflow together by moving as much of the

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:19.270
reaction as possible into a low cost single use cartridge that still meets analytical performance

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:25.490
standards. That move requires demonstrating equivalent accuracy and precision under CLIA

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:31.370
waived or near-waived conditions so that the test can be performed closer to the patient and without

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:37.770
the full central lab overhead. Second, the cartridge would need to be manufactured at volumes that

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:44.110
drive the per-unit cost of plastics and reagents toward their commodity floor. Achieving that scale

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:49.630
depends on standardized interfaces that multiple instrument makers could adopt rather than proprietary

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:56.090
lock-in. Third, transparent pricing and direct reimbursement for the cartridge itself rather than

00:06:56.210 --> 00:07:01.550
bundled lab fees would remove the administrative layer that currently multiplies the ticket price.

00:07:02.350 --> 00:07:07.930
Each of these steps faces genuine constraints. Analytical performance cannot be traded away without

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:14.450
creating clinical risk. Regulators rightly demand evidence that simplified devices do not increase

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:20.930
false positives or negatives. Supply chain concentration and high-grade plastics and enzymes could

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:27.290
create new bottlenecks if demand surged. Liability allocation remains difficult when a result is

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:32.810
generated outside a traditional laboratory setting. Yet none of these obstacles changes the arithmetic

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:39.150
that the physical inputs are already cheap. The prize is therefore the reduction of everything that

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:45.250
sits between those inputs and the final report. The first concrete signal that someone is pursuing this

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:50.870
path would be a cartridge whose published bill of materials is openly compared against its selling

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:58.110
price, making the new idiot index visible rather than opaque. A test whose price greatly exceeds its

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:03.290
reagents is announcing that the design of the surrounding system, not the chemistry, is the dominant

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:10.150
variable. Questioning the requirement for central lab processing before optimizing the next analyzer

00:08:10.150 --> 00:08:16.250
is the move that opens the largest reduction. Tomorrow, the show returns with another example or

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:22.590
opportunity drawn from the same principle. The question left on the table is which diagnostic category

00:08:22.590 --> 00:08:28.990
will first publish its own reagent to build price ratio and treat the gap as an engineering target

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:34.970
rather than an accepted cost of doing business. That's first principles daily for today. If this

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:39.710
changed how you see what things cost share it with someone who'd appreciate it and subscribe wherever

00:08:39.710 --> 00:08:46.470
you listen. I'm Patrick in Vancouver. One example or one opportunity every day. See you tomorrow.

